ABSTRACT
================================================================================

HCSM-22 constructs the zero state s as the unique nullvector of the 14x19
measurement matrix M of vacuum-native observables, but explicitly does not
select s: the zero state is stated there as "a structural fact about the
multiplet and the observable set, not a selection rule." This sub-paper
supplies the missing selection rule.

We prove that the vacuum is the state of the 14-mode multiplet at the
self-paired eigenvalue lambda = 4 that minimizes the total squared expectation
value of the 19 observables, i.e.

    s = argmin_{||s||=1} ||M^T s||^2.

Two independent derivations are given, both from the substrate.

First, the LANDAUER ROUTE: the processor T of HCSM-13 operates at the
deterministic Landauer bound (HCSM-21, Thm. 12.1), the vacuum is the state of
minimum information, and minimum information is exactly M^T s = 0.

Second, the ENTROPY-WELL ROUTE: identifying the dimensional deviation
epsilon = D - 4 of HCSM-54 with the observable magnitude ||M^T s||, the vacuum
entropy well becomes

    S(s)/S_0 = 1 - gamma*||M^T s||^2 + O(||M^T s||^4),

which is maximized exactly when M^T s = 0.

The two routes are dual descriptions of the same physical fact: the vacuum is
the state from which the machine extracts zero information. The uniqueness of
the maximizer is established from rank(M) = 13 and nullity(M^T) = 1 (HCSM-22,
Thm. 4). Item 2.1 of the HCSM derivation list (Zero State Selection Principle)
is closed.

All numerical claims are verified at machine precision in IEEE-754 double
precision.

