1.1 The zero state problem

The Hodge Complex Standard Model (HCSM) identifies the 14 modes at the
self-paired eigenvalue lambda = 4 of the discrete 5-point Laplacian on the
periodic 8x8 torus Lambda = Z_8 x Z_8 with one Standard Model generation
[HCSM-00, HCSM-04, HCSM-23].

HCSM-22 constructs the zero state: the unique configuration s of the 14-mode
multiplet consistent with the simultaneous vanishing of 19 vacuum-native
observables. The 19 observables form a 14x19 real measurement matrix M, whose
transpose has rank 13 and nullity 1. The unique nullvector s (up to scale) is
the zero state.

HCSM-22 establishes the following structural properties of s:

  (i)   s is odd under the chiral reflection rs: s(rs(m)) = -s(m) for all 14
        modes (Thm. 8).

  (ii)  The two rs-fixed modes W = (2,2) and t = (6,6) have exactly zero
        amplitude (Cor. 9).

  (iii) The six independent amplitudes a_1, ..., a_6 satisfy the exact
        algebraic ratios a_3 = 2*a_4 and a_5 = (2 - sqrt(2))*a_4 (Thm. 12).

  (iv)  86.5% of the |s|-mass sits on the middle D4 orbit O_1 (Thm. 17).

HCSM-22 explicitly states: "The zero state is a structural fact about the
multiplet and the observable set, not a selection rule." The construction is
complete; the selection is not.

This sub-paper supplies the selection rule. The physical principle is:

    The vacuum is the state of minimum information: the state from which
    the processor T extracts zero information.

Two independent derivations of this principle are given. Both are HCSM-native.
Both select the same zero state, and the equivalence of the two routes is a
structural theorem, not a coincidence.

