4. THE LANDAUER ROUTE
================================================================================

Theorem 4.1 (Landauer selection of the zero state). The processor T operates
at the deterministic Landauer bound. The vacuum is the state of minimum
information. Minimum information is exactly M^T s = 0. Hence the vacuum is the
zero state.

Proof. Three steps.

(i) The machine operates at the Landauer bound. By HCSM-21 Thm. 12.1, the
loop-pass duration is T_DME = 1/(2*gamma), the unique dissipative scale in
HCSM. By HCSM-13 Prop. 3.4, the processor reduces the entropy of the maximally
mixed state on the 56-dimensional Hodge sector to the entropy of the maximally
mixed state on the fermion-boson product 10 + 4:

    Delta S = log 56 - log 40 = log(7/5).

This saturates the deterministic Landauer bound.

(ii) The machine reads the multiplet through the 19 observables. The
measurement matrix M_{i,a} = O_a(m_i) encodes the machine's readout of the
multiplet. The expectation value of observable O_a in a state s is

    <O_a>_s = sum_i O_a(m_i) s_i = (M^T s)_a.

(iii) The vacuum is the state of minimum information. A state s with
<O_a>_s != 0 for some a is a state from which the machine extracts
information. The vacuum is the state from which no information is extracted:

    <O_a>_s = 0   for all a = 1, ..., 19.

This is exactly M^T s = 0. By HCSM-22 Thm. 4 and Cor. 2.3, the unique
solution is the zero state.  QED

Remark 4.2. The Landauer route uses only the machine's operation. It does not
require the entropy-well structure of HCSM-54. The two routes (this section
and Section 5) are independent derivations of the same selection.

