6. EQUIVALENCE OF THE TWO ROUTES
================================================================================

Theorem 6.1 (Route equivalence). The Landauer route (Thm. 4.1) and the
entropy-well route (Thm. 5.1) give the same selection. They are dual
descriptions of the same minimum-information principle.

Proof. The Landauer route selects the state with <O_a>_s = 0 for all a, i.e.
M^T s = 0. The entropy-well route selects the maximizer of
1 - gamma*||M^T s||^2, i.e. M^T s = 0. The two conditions are identical, and
both reduce to the same nullvector condition. The physical content is the same:
the vacuum is the state from which the machine extracts zero information
(Landauer language) or equivalently the state of maximum entropy in the
entropy well (thermodynamic language).  QED

Remark 6.2 (Structural content). The equivalence is not tautological. The
Landauer route uses only the operation of the processor T (a projection
determined by the winding class partition), while the entropy-well route uses
the self-dual modular structure of the square torus (via gamma). That these
two independent structures select the same state is a non-trivial consistency
check on the framework. It is the substrate-level statement that the machine's
operation and the vacuum's thermodynamic structure agree.

