12. CONCLUSION
================================================================================

This sub-paper has supplied the missing selection rule for the zero state of
HCSM-22. The vacuum is the state of minimum information: the state from which
the processor T extracts zero information. Two independent derivations
establish this.

The Landauer route uses only the operation of the processor T at the
deterministic Landauer bound (HCSM-21). The entropy-well route uses the
self-dual modular structure of the square torus (via gamma of HCSM-54). Both
select the same state, M^T s = 0, which is the HCSM-22 zero state. The
equivalence of the two routes is a structural consistency check: the machine's
operation and the vacuum's thermodynamic structure agree at the substrate
level.

The selection principle is

    s = argmin_{||s||=1} ||M^T s||^2,

and the state-dependent entropy well is

    S(s)/S_0 = 1 - gamma*||M^T s||^2 + O(||M^T s||^4).

The uniqueness of the maximizer follows from rank(M) = 13 and nullity(M^T) = 1
(HCSM-22, Thm. 4). Item 2.1 of the HCSM derivation list (Zero State Selection
Principle) is closed.

The structural properties of s established in HCSM-22 (rs-oddness, vanishing
at {W,t}, exact ratios, weight fractions) and in HCSM-05 (closed forms of a_2,
a_4, a_6 in Q(sqrt(2))) are inherited. The role of s as the sort key of the
charge table (HCSM-24 revised, Thm. 10.1) is inherited.

The remaining open items are technical: the exact closed-form reconstruction
of the 8 non-verifiable columns of M, and the identification of the bridge
constant in epsilon = c*||M^T s|| beyond the normalization choice c = 1.
Neither is required for the selection principle itself.

  Physics is what the machine cannot cancel. The Hodge complex is the machine.
  The vacuum is the state from which the machine extracts nothing.

